<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <UserSecretsId>14042f2d-7ba1-46b8-8cb9-8bc29e68213b</UserSecretsId>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- This project's container image properties (ContainerImageName/Tag, an Alpine base image)
       were copied in from day-5/piece2 along with the rest of QuotesApi, for that piece's own
       manual "dotnet publish /t:PublishContainer" + "docker run" workflow. Both broke real,
       live azd deployment in this piece, confirmed by actually running azd deploy against a real
       Azure Container Apps environment:
       1. The Alpine base image expects musl libc, but azd's own container build resolved a
          glibc-linked SQLitePCLRaw native library into it regardless (no exposed way to pin the
          RID azd's build uses) - the deployed revision crash-looped with "Error loading shared
          library libe_sqlite3: No such file or directory".
       2. ContainerImageName=quotes-api forces the pushed repository name to "quotes-api", but
          azd deploy separately updates the Container App resource to reference azd's own computed
          repository path ("piece4/quotes-api-<env-name>") - a path nothing was ever pushed to,
          so the container entered ImagePullBackOff.
       azd fully owns image naming and the container's target platform for this piece's actual
       exercise (deploy via azd CLI, no Dockerfile) - neither property is needed here, so both are
       removed rather than reconciled against azd's own conventions. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" Version="10.0.10">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite" Version="10.0.10" />
    <!-- Reads APPLICATIONINSIGHTS_CONNECTION_STRING (set by azd's Bicep) and wires ASP.NET Core
         request tracing into Application Insights - see Program.cs for why this was missing. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Azure.Monitor.OpenTelemetry.AspNetCore" Version="1.6.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
